---
import Layout from '~/layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { createBreadcrumbSchema } from '~/utils/structured-data';

const metadata = {
  title: 'Engineering Content and Editorial Policy',
  description:
    'How TITANIUM 3DP researches, reviews, sources, labels, updates, consolidates, and corrects titanium additive manufacturing content.',
  structuredData: createBreadcrumbSchema([
    { name: 'Home', item: '/' },
    { name: 'Engineering content policy', item: '/editorial-policy/' },
  ]),
};
---

<Layout metadata={metadata}>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-12 sm:px-6 sm:py-16 lg:py-20">
    <header class="max-w-3xl">
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-primary">Trust and methodology</p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 font-heading text-4xl font-bold leading-tight text-heading sm:text-5xl">
        Engineering content and editorial policy
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-8 text-muted">
        TITANIUM 3DP publishes practical guidance for engineers and buyers evaluating titanium additive manufacturing.
        The purpose is to reduce material, process, qualification, and purchasing mistakes before production files are
        submitted.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose mt-10 max-w-none dark:prose-invert dark:prose-headings:text-heading prose-a:text-link">
      <h2>Who prepares the content</h2>
      <p>
        Articles are published under <strong>TITANIUM 3DP Engineering</strong>. Content is prepared from manufacturing
        constraints, supplier-route review, project requirements, standards, regulatory or government guidance,
        peer-reviewed work, and other public technical sources relevant to titanium LPBF, EBM, post-processing, and
        finished-part acceptance. Commercial inquiries and production coordination are handled by Suzhou Como Precision
        Materials Co., Ltd.
      </p>

      <h2>How technical claims are handled</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Material claims identify the titanium grade, condition, or governing route where practical.</li>
        <li>
          Property values are not universal guarantees for every machine, parameter set, build orientation, coupon, or
          finished part.
        </li>
        <li>
          Process guidance separates printable geometry from heat treatment, support removal, machining, cleaning,
          inspection, and release.
        </li>
        <li>
          Standards and external sources provide context; project acceptance values still come from the drawing,
          specification, purchase order, and approved qualification plan.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>High-risk applications</h2>
      <p>
        Aerospace, defense, pressure, energy, medical, and other regulated or safety-relevant pages define the boundary
        between general engineering feasibility and program-specific approval. A material standard does not certify the
        complete part, supplier route, cleaning process, device, or production program.
      </p>

      <h2>Cases, scenarios, and capability statements</h2>
      <p>
        A named customer result is described as a customer case only when publication rights and supporting evidence are
        available. Otherwise, an article may use an <strong>illustrative engineering review scenario</strong>. Such a
        scenario must not be read as proof of a specific customer, machine run, measured result, certification, or
        production approval. Supplier-specific equipment, build envelope, quality-system, or certification claims are
        published only after they are verified.
      </p>

      <h2>Images</h2>
      <p>
        Images are used when they clarify a part type, geometry, process step, failure mode, or inspection decision.
        Decorative image count is not a publishing target. Illustrative or generated imagery must not be presented as
        production evidence.
      </p>

      <h2>New-page and consolidation rules</h2>
      <p>
        A new URL must answer a distinct material, process, application, failure, acceptance, or purchasing decision.
        Keyword variants alone do not justify a page. Existing URLs are preferred when they can be expanded without
        changing search intent. Overlapping pages may be rewritten, consolidated, or redirected after page and query
        evidence is reviewed.
      </p>

      <h2>Updates and corrections</h2>
      <p>
        Substantive revisions receive an updated date. Dates are not changed merely to make content appear fresh.
        Corrections should preserve the affected claim, source boundary, and commercial implication rather than silently
        replacing one unsupported statement with another.
      </p>

      <h2>What this website does not replace</h2>
      <p>
        Website guidance is not a manufacturing guarantee, certified material report, simulation, regulatory opinion,
        safety assessment, or acceptance plan for a specific part. A quotation requires the CAD model, drawing,
        quantity, operating requirements, material preference if known, and the inspection or documentation scope that
        matters.
      </p>

      <p>
        Corrections and technical questions can be sent to
        <a href="mailto:info@szcomo.com">info@szcomo.com</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
